WEBVTT

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:02.300
Every value can say where it came from.

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:05.333
Now make sure each customer
is only one record.

00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:07.533
Two records for one
customer means two scores,

00:00:07.533 --> 00:00:09.633
two owners and two cold emails.

00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:12.933
By the end you'll know how
rules decide who is the same.

00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:14.100
You'll merge a pair, undo it,

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.867
and see why a correction
survives the next sync.

00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:20.367
Leo imports a list, and
Northwind now exists twice.

00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:24.500
One copy sits with Sophia's renewal
rep. The other sits in a cold sequence.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:29.167
Same customer, two owners, one
awkward email. The fix has four parts.

00:00:29.167 --> 00:00:33.533
Merge by signed rules. Approve with
a second person. Undo anything.

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.700
And a human's value always wins.

00:00:35.733 --> 00:00:36.933
First, the rule.

00:00:36.933 --> 00:00:39.067
An identity rule is a
signed definition that

00:00:39.067 --> 00:00:41.100
says which keys make two records the same.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.267
Companies match on domain, contacts
on primary email, with weights,

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:47.800
a minimum confidence and
a name similarity floor.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.000
If you haven't signed one, GTM OS
uses built in values and says so.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.000
The same rule drives the duplicate scan

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.433
and the resolver that
runs when data arrives.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:01.067
Matching goes in order: exact keys,
then blocking keys, then probability.

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:05.067
If a match isn't safe to decide, the
pair waits as a resolution review.

00:01:05.067 --> 00:01:07.333
In the console: Operate, Needs a human.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:11.067
A person links them, keeps a new
record, or rejects the match.

00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:12.333
Nothing is guessed.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.267
Second, scan and merge.

00:01:14.267 --> 00:01:17.133
In the console: Engines,
Merges, Duplicate candidates.

00:01:17.133 --> 00:01:20.333
Rescan runs the scan again.
Each pair shows why it matched,

00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:23.967
with a confidence score and
both records side by side.

00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:28.500
Pick the survivor, and see which empty
fields it will fill from the other.

00:01:28.533 --> 00:01:30.733
Merge proposes. It doesn't merge.

00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:34.367
The card lists each conflicting field
and the strategy that settles it.

00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:38.667
Keep the survivor, most recent,
most trusted source, most complete,

00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:41.733
first written, highest
confidence, or a person picks.

00:01:41.733 --> 00:01:45.667
A different team member approves and
executes before any record changes,

00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:48.567
and the duplicate is
archived, not deleted.

00:01:48.633 --> 00:01:51.167
If you disagree with a
call, pick the other value.

00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:54.733
The revised proposal goes back to
someone who didn't propose or revise it.

00:01:54.733 --> 00:01:58.333
And if the survivor changed in the
meantime, the merge refuses to run,

00:01:58.333 --> 00:02:00.367
so a stale decision never lands.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.100
Or ask your agent: "Find duplicate
accounts and propose merges".

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.133
It proposes the pairs it's
confident about, one approval each,

00:02:07.133 --> 00:02:09.200
and leaves the doubtful pairs for you.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.500
It can also preview a single merge first,

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:14.767
showing the filled fields and how
each conflict would be settled.

00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:19.800
Third, undo. In the console: Engines,
Merges, Recent merges, then Unmerge.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.767
Unmerge is its own proposal
and needs approval too.

00:02:22.767 --> 00:02:25.133
It brings back the archived
record and restores

00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:27.600
only the fields that still
hold the merged value.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.633
Edits made since the
merge are never trampled.

00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:32.400
Fourth, corrections that stick.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:36.167
A value a person sets, including
a pick made during a merge,

00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:40.367
is recorded in field history. Survivorship
never overwrites a human value.

00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:44.367
So the next CRM sync, or a rebuild
of the records from their sources,

00:02:44.367 --> 00:02:46.067
doesn't put the old one back.

00:02:46.133 --> 00:02:48.567
The Merge rules card, on the same page,

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:52.233
sets how each conflicting field is
settled, with a default for the rest.

00:02:52.233 --> 00:02:53.467
Saving changes nothing live.

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:57.600
It proposes a new version of the identity
rule, signed like any definition.

00:02:57.633 --> 00:03:02.067
Last, the Data quality tab. In the
console: Engines, Merges, Data quality.

00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:04.400
It shows a quality score, the duplicates,

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:07.667
the stale records and how many were
scanned, then each field's fill rate,

00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:11.200
lowest first. That tells
you where to clean next.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:14.167
Monitors watch bounce and
complaint rates and freshness,

00:03:14.167 --> 00:03:16.533
and a nightly scan keeps it all current.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:17.800
Or ask your agent:

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.767
"Give me the data quality report
before and after this import".

00:03:20.767 --> 00:03:23.933
Pro move: run it before every
big import and again after.

00:03:23.933 --> 00:03:26.667
The difference is what the
import did to your fill rate,

00:03:26.667 --> 00:03:27.867
duplicates and staleness.

00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:31.167
Any cleanup that follows is
a proposal a person approves.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.833
A careful engineer will say: an
approval per pair will bury my team.

00:03:34.833 --> 00:03:37.267
Merges are proposed one
pair at a time today.

00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:40.167
So the agent proposes only the
pairs it's confident about,

00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:41.900
and leaves the doubtful ones for you.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:45.900
And every merge can be undone, so
a wrong call costs one unmerge.

00:03:45.967 --> 00:03:48.800
Checkpoint: A signed rule
decides who is the same.

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:50.767
A second person approves every merge.

00:03:50.767 --> 00:03:54.233
Unmerge brings it back, and
a human's value stays put.

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:59.000
One customer, one record, every merge
reversible, and corrections that stay.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:02.000
Run Rescan on Duplicate candidates
and read the first pair.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.800
Coming next: ask your data
anything, and what it remembers.
